# 2 GB swap file.
#
# The instance is a t3.micro: 2 vCPU but only 1 GiB of RAM, because t3.micro is
# the only Free Tier EC2 size. That is not enough headroom for what happens on
# an app restart, and on 29 Jul 2026 it cost a 30-minute API outage.
#
# Setting a single environment variable makes Elastic Beanstalk restart the
# application. On this box that restart wedged the instance outright: it stopped
# reporting health after ~90 seconds, half the ELB requests began returning 5xx,
# EB burned its 15-minute timeout, reverted the configuration, then sat in
# Updating for another 20 minutes -- which also blocks every EB operation, so
# restart-app-server refused with "Must be Ready". The only way out was to go
# around EB entirely with `aws ec2 reboot-instances`.
#
# It was not throttling and not a dead VM: EC2 reported running/ok throughout
# and CPU credits were barely touched (270 of 288). CPU sat at ~50% -- one of
# the two vCPUs pegged -- from the instant the update began, which is what
# memory pressure looks like on a box this size. `sharp` builds native binaries
# here (see AGENTS.md §9: it must be built on Amazon Linux, never zipped from a
# dev machine), and 1 GiB does not comfortably do that while also serving.
#
# Swap does not make the instance fast. It makes it degrade instead of dying,
# which is the difference between a slow restart and an outage nobody can
# intervene in.
#
# Remove this once the environment moves to an instance with real memory.
# AGENTS.md records t3.small (2 GiB) as the minimum for the client's own
# account, where no Free Tier constraint applies.
commands:
  01_create_swap:
    # fallocate is refused on some Amazon Linux filesystems; dd always works.
    test: test ! -f /var/swapfile
    command: |
      dd if=/dev/zero of=/var/swapfile bs=1M count=2048
      chmod 600 /var/swapfile
      mkswap /var/swapfile
  02_enable_swap:
    # swapon is not idempotent -- it errors if the file is already active, and a
    # non-zero exit here fails the whole deployment.
    test: test -f /var/swapfile && ! swapon -s | grep -q /var/swapfile
    command: swapon /var/swapfile
  03_persist_swap:
    test: ! grep -q /var/swapfile /etc/fstab
    command: echo '/var/swapfile none swap sw 0 0' >> /etc/fstab
